## 3. Specify and estimate competing models

We will fit an intercept-only model and three increasingly complex substantive models.

| Model | Formula | Research rationale |
|:--|:--|:--|
| M0: Null | `rating ~ 1` | Baseline model |
| M1: Education | `rating ~ complaints` | Rating varies with complaints |
| M2: Education + Income | `rating ~ complaints + learning` | Learning explains additional variation |
| M3: Expanded | `rating ~ complaints + learning + raises` | Raises explains additional variation |

The models are **nested**: each successive model contains all predictors from the preceding model, plus one or more additional predictors.

```{r}
#| label: fit-models

# Intercept-only model
mod0 <- lm(rating ~ 1, data = dat)

# Complaints model
mod1 <- lm(rating ~ complaints, data = dat)

# Complaints + learning model
mod2 <- lm(rating ~ complaints + learning, data = dat)

# Expanded model
mod3 <- lm(rating ~ complaints + learning + raises, data = dat)
```

### Inspecting the models

After executing the model-fitting code, explore the results in the **R Console**:

```r
summary(mod0)
summary(mod1)
summary(mod2)
summary(mod3)

confint(mod3)
```

Some important interpretive distinctions:

- In M0, the intercept represents the sample mean workplace rating score.
- In M1, the complaints coefficient represents the expected difference in workplace rating associated with handling scores of employee complaints
- In M2, the complaints coefficient represents this association after adjusting for opportunity to learn.
- In M3, complaints and learning coefficients are additionally adjusted for pay increases.


Examine the coding using:

```r
levels(dat$type)
contrasts(dat$type)
```

